-- Auto-generated backfill for contacts reachable via LinkedIn/email but missing
-- outreach message content, sourced from the published RevHub-Marketing sheet
-- (https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1BOTCJTXNPOwUVB9BmILDF4xgDavNOWVGQJSHQCcxy3M).
-- Uses coalesce() throughout so it only fills genuinely empty fields, never
-- overwrites existing content.

update public.project_contacts set
  linkedin_connect_message = coalesce(linkedin_connect_message, $$Hi Tom, I saw 360training's Head of Analytics search. The mandate to integrate 18 acquisitions, modernize the data foundation for AI, and improve company-wide decisions closely matches transformation work I have led. I would value connecting.$$),
  intro_message = coalesce(intro_message, $$Thanks for connecting, Tom. The Head of Analytics role signals a broader transformation than dashboard modernization. 360training needs a shared decision system across acquired brands, customer and course performance, marketing, sales, operations, finance, and technology. I have led similar programs across fragmented CRM, marketing automation, customer, call-center, operational, and analytics systems, while my partner Harsh brings deeper data architecture and modeling. We could help define the commercial and executive layer around the build so the internal analytics team starts with clear business priorities and source-of-truth rules. Our approach is here: https://www.aboutchad.com/approach$$),
  follow_up_message = coalesce(follow_up_message, $$Tom, a practical first phase could map the highest-value executive questions to the required entities, metrics, source systems, acquisition-integration gaps, forecast inputs, and activation use cases. That would give the analytics team a prioritized roadmap and help leadership see value before the entire AI-enabled foundation is complete.$$)
where lower(contact_name) = lower('Tom Anderson') and company = '360training';

update public.project_contacts set
  linkedin_connect_message = coalesce(linkedin_connect_message, $$Hi Ryan, 360training's Head of Analytics search caught my attention. Your mandate across marketing, business development, and B2B sales makes the need to connect multi-brand customer data, acquisition, pipeline, retention, and investment decisions especially relevant. I would value connecting.$$),
  intro_message = coalesce(intro_message, $$Thanks for connecting, Ryan. I am interested in how 360training is creating one commercial view across 18 acquisitions, multiple training brands, ecommerce behavior, digital marketing, B2B sales, customer lifecycle performance, and profitability. I have led similar work across customer databases, CRM, marketing automation, analytics, segmentation, and revenue reporting, including helping scale digital revenue from $400M to $1.25B at QXO. I would be glad to compare approaches and explore where RevHub could complement the internal analytics build with commercial architecture and activation capacity. Our approach is here: https://www.aboutchad.com/approach$$),
  follow_up_message = coalesce(follow_up_message, $$Ryan, one useful starting point could be a portfolio growth map connecting each brand and customer segment to acquisition source, course or product mix, first conversion, repeat purchase, B2B pipeline, retention, margin, and cross-sell potential. That would make the analytics investment immediately useful for marketing and commercial prioritization.$$)
where lower(contact_name) = lower('Ryan Linders') and company = '360training';

update public.project_contacts set
  linkedin_connect_message = coalesce(linkedin_connect_message, $$Hi Marie, I saw AIRA's fractional COO search. As Board President, your oversight of the organization's strategy and sustainability makes the operating-model, resource, and performance mandate especially relevant. I would value connecting.$$),
  intro_message = coalesce(intro_message, $$Thanks for connecting, Marie. AIRA's fractional COO mandate sits at the intersection of governance, organizational sustainability, operating performance, resource allocation, and executive accountability. I have led transformation work that connects strategy, workflows, data, KPIs, forecasting, and management cadence into one operating model. I could help the board and leadership team establish a practical foundation that improves execution without creating unnecessary bureaucracy. My approach is here: https://www.aboutchad.com/approach$$),
  follow_up_message = coalesce(follow_up_message, $$Marie, one useful first phase would map AIRA's strategic priorities to funding, programs, capacity, decision rights, risks, and measurable outcomes. That would give the board and executive team a clear transformation roadmap and a shared view of whether the COO engagement is improving organizational health.$$)
where lower(contact_name) = lower('Marie Bottomley Hartel') and company = 'American Immunization Registry Association';

update public.project_contacts set
  linkedin_connect_message = coalesce(linkedin_connect_message, $$Hi Steve, I saw AIRA's fractional COO search. Your role as Board Treasurer makes the connection between financial governance, operating capacity, resource allocation, and long-term sustainability especially relevant. I would value connecting.$$),
  intro_message = coalesce(intro_message, $$Thanks for connecting, Steve. AIRA's COO mandate includes the areas where financial governance and operations must reinforce one another: resource allocation, program commitments, capacity, performance measures, revenue diversification, and long-term sustainability. I have built operating and commercial systems that connect planning, workflows, data, forecasting, and executive reporting, and would be glad to compare approaches that give a board clearer visibility without burdening the staff. My approach is here: https://www.aboutchad.com/approach$$),
  follow_up_message = coalesce(follow_up_message, $$Steve, a practical starting point could be a board-level operating and financial model connecting funding sources, program obligations, staffing capacity, major risks, scenario assumptions, and measurable outcomes. That would make it easier to evaluate tradeoffs and the value created by the fractional COO engagement.$$)
where lower(contact_name) = lower('Steve Murchie') and company = 'American Immunization Registry Association';

update public.project_contacts set
  linkedin_connect_message = coalesce(linkedin_connect_message, $$Hi Amanda, your work taking BleuBully from a product idea to a sold-out D2C brand caught my attention. I build commercial systems across segmentation, lifecycle, analytics, forecasting, and revenue operations, and would value connecting and comparing approaches.$$),
  intro_message = coalesce(intro_message, $$Thanks for connecting, Amanda. BleuBully appears to be moving from successful launch validation into the harder phase of repeatable scale across product expansion, paid and organic acquisition, website conversion, retention, wholesale, and contribution economics. I have spent more than 15 years building the connective layer across customer segmentation, CRM, campaigns, analytics, forecasting, and executive reporting, while my partner Harsh brings deeper data architecture and modeling. I would be interested in comparing notes on the growth questions you are solving and where complementary RevHub capacity could strengthen the operating model behind the brand. Our approach is here: https://www.aboutchad.com/approach$$),
  follow_up_message = coalesce(follow_up_message, $$Amanda, one useful discussion may be how BleuBully connects audience and creative tests to first-order margin, repeat purchase, product affinity, wholesale potential, and inventory decisions. A shared commercial model across those signals can help distinguish short-term demand from the customer and channel patterns worth scaling.$$),
  email_subject = coalesce(email_subject, $$A commercial operating layer for BleuBully's next stage$$),
  email_intro_message = coalesce(email_intro_message, $$Hi Amanda,

Your work taking BleuBully from a product idea to a sold-out D2C brand stood out. The next phase appears materially different from launch: product and color expansion, acquisition efficiency, website conversion, lifecycle growth, wholesale opportunities, inventory decisions, and a clearer view of contribution economics.

I have spent more than 15 years building commercial systems that connect customer segmentation, campaigns, CRM, lifecycle behavior, forecasting, analytics, and executive reporting. At QXO, I helped scale digital revenue from $400M to $1.25B while unifying fragmented customer, marketing, call-center, and operational data. Through RevHub, my partner Harsh and I add the strategy and analytical depth needed to turn growth activity into a shared operating model.

For BleuBully, a practical first step could connect audience and creative tests to first-order margin, product affinity, repeat purchase, wholesale potential, and inventory implications. That would help clarify which customer and channel patterns are worth scaling as the assortment expands.

Would you be open to a 20-minute conversation to compare approaches? Our framework is here: https://www.aboutchad.com/approach

Best,
Chad Parker$$),
  email_follow_up_1 = coalesce(email_follow_up_1, $$Hi Amanda,

One concrete deliverable could be a customer and channel growth map connecting acquisition source, creative message, first purchase, product mix, margin, repeat behavior, and wholesale potential. It would show where BleuBully is generating durable customer value versus short-term demand.

Would a brief conversation next week be useful?$$),
  email_follow_up_2 = coalesce(email_follow_up_2, $$Hi Amanda,

Closing the loop. BleuBully already has the brand story and early market proof. The opportunity I see is building the commercial measurement and decision system that helps the team scale acquisition, retention, assortment, and wholesale from the same evidence.

Is there a 20-minute window worth holding, or is another stakeholder closer to that work?$$)
where lower(contact_name) = lower('Amanda Pond') and company = 'BleuBully Bedding';

